}

data declaration_index_kernel_named_import_escape_note: String = "THE ONE HOLE THE IMPORT-MEMBER ARM DOES NOT CLOSE, HELD OPEN DELIBERATELY AND HELD COUNTABLE. v1.compiler.resolve get_exported_names builds a module's export surface as item names, variant names, the names that module itself LISTS in its own import blocks, and every key of kernel_type_set. The last term means EVERY module in the corpus exports every kernel type name, so the claim import m -- Int -- is admitted whatever m declares. The index mirrors that rather than refusing it, because refusing source the seed compiler accepts is a change to what the seed ACCEPTS, and editing get_exported_names classifies as NewLanguageBehavior, which the v1 freeze refuses and which a refusal dominates every admission of.\n\nMEASURED AGAINST THE INSTALLED COMPILER, not inferred from the code. On a throwaway source root, a module whose entire body is one fn returning Int was imported as import extdeps.probe_missing_anchor -- Int, String, Bool -- and compiled to 6 files with 0 diagnostics. THE ZERO IS READABLE BECAUSE THE NONZERO WAS RUN BESIDE IT: the same source root with a member named probe_absent_member_RED refused with a located MissingExport at the member token, 0 files emitted. So the wall is live and this class walks through it.\n\nTHE SPECIMEN IS NOT SYNTHETIC, and it is a finding in its own right rather than a count. std.types declares no Int, no String and no Float anywhere -- it names them as KEYS of kernel_type_set, which is a different fact -- and hundreds of modules author import std.types -- Int, String. Every one of those claims is false about std.types and always has been. The reason nobody noticed is precisely the escape: the claim is unfalsifiable, so its frequency is zero by construction.\n\nWHAT THIS CHANGE DOES ABOUT IT, which is the only thing DESIGN section 5 permits an arm that widens to do: make the frequency OBSERVABLE. A bare continue would have zeroed the deficit exactly as the corpus does, so the class could never rank for repair -- section 6 prices by displaced cost and a masked cost displaces nothing. It is now counted as import_members_kernel_named on DeclarationIndexPopulation and printed beside every other denominator in both the required-ci and the standalone sweep lines, so a green names it. It is NOT reported as a finding and does not stop the line.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE IS AT THE FIXTURE BOUNDARY, where DESIGN section 4b requires it and where the corpus cannot reach: kernel_named_import_member_is_admitted_but_counted plants the claim and requires BOTH no finding AND a count of exactly one, and a_declared_member_does_not_count_as_kernel_named requires a real member to leave the counter at zero. The first assertion alone would be satisfied by re-silencing the escape; the pair is what makes that a RED.\n\nNEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: get_exported_names grounds its export surface in DEFINITIONS rather than appending the kernel set to every module, at which point import_member_is_kernel_named deletes and the members it admits become ordinary ImportMemberAbsent findings. That is a v1 language-behavior change and is out of scope here; this row is what keeps it ranked."

data declaration_index_authorship_arm_is_not_a_mandatory_tag_region_note: String = "WHY THE LENS AUTHORSHIP ARM IS HOST-SIDE RATHER THAN A SECOND MandatoryTagRegion, recorded because it is the first thing a reviewer will propose and because the reason is a measurement rather than a preference.\n\nv2.lens.mandatory_tag already models exactly this shape: a MandatoryTagRegion names a dotted prefix, a required declaration name and a required type name, and mandatory_tag_law states that a violating module FAILS COMPILATION with a typed, located diagnostic -- unwritable at definition admission, not flagged post-hoc by a corpus scan. mandatory_tag_lens is enrolled in v2.compiler.00_compile always_required_root_lenses. So the obvious move is one more region with prefix v2.lens., required declaration construction_justification, required type ConstructionJustification.\n\nIT DOES NOT FIRE. Measured on the installed compiler: a module declaring itself extdeps.probe_missing_anchor, carrying no extdeps_external_authority_anchor and matched by none of that region's exempt_modules, exempt_module_prefixes or exempt_module_suffixes rows, compiled to 5 files with 0 diagnostics. The v2 compile door is not on the gunbc compile path; the executing compiler is the v1 seed. The zero is readable because the same source root produced a located MissingExport refusal in the control described in the sibling row above, so the compiler does refuse at ingestion -- this gate is simply not reached.\n\nWHAT IS AND IS NOT CLAIMED. This is a note about REACHABILITY of one gate from one path, and it is not a defect finding filed against v2.lens.mandatory_tag, whose fold is correct and whose own witness exercises it. It is the reason the authorship obligation is answered here instead: an arm enrolled in a door that the required run never opens would be green by construction, which DESIGN section 4b calls worse than absent because it gets cited as coverage. When the v2 compile door is the executing ingestion, this arm is the one that should dissolve into a MandatoryTagRegion, and that is its next rung."
